# Myth or History?
Tamil title: புராணமா? சரித்திரமா?

This explanation in response to what Comrade P. Sri wrote about the *Periyapuranam* in *Dinamani* has continued over the past two weeks and concludes in this issue.

Some may ask: “No, what you say is inappropriate. Siva, who came in the guise of an ascetic, came only to test Tiruneelakantar's devotion, not to teach the world the ascetic state. How, then, can your statement be fitting?” That is true. But my question concerns the lesson readers can learn from Sekkizhar's account of what occurred between Tiruneelakantar and Siva in ascetic guise.

This is no age in which everything seen or heard is believed and accepted exactly as given. In a time that penetrates and analyzes everything by asking whether it can be accepted, whether it can be believed, whether it contains truth and whether it accords with reason, I ask readers to consider what conclusion people will reach upon reading Tiruneelakantar's story.

Moreover, this is an age when Siva has entirely abandoned his former habit of repeatedly appearing as a mendicant or with the Goddess as half his body to test the love of his Tamil devotees. It is an age when people, distressed that Siva and the other gods have disappeared and cannot be seen, employ English researchers to excavate the mounds at Harappa, Mohenjo-daro and elsewhere. Why have these people still not understood how ridiculous it is in this age to recount stories of gods who came to test ancient devotees, explain them, deliver lectures upon them and draw pictures of them? That is my concern.

We are next told that the Saivite ascetic who brought a case against Tiruneelakantar went to the Brahmins of Tillai to state his complaint. Where should a person prosecuting an accused man present his case? Everyone would say that the proper course is to present it before a court. If the accusation is not grave enough for a court, the matter should be stated to the village panchayat for decision. If it is too slight even for a panchayat, the proper course is to approach the accused man's relatives and ask, “This is the wrong your man has done me; what judgment do you give?”

Sometimes the accused belongs to one community and the complainant to another. If the case is placed before the accused man's community, communal attachment may distort the proceeding and judgment. Minor complaints of this kind may therefore be submitted to people belonging to neither community.

But none of these methods was followed in the case before us. The complainant was a Brahmin ascetic, the accused a man of the potter caste, and the judges Brahmins of Tillai. Yet the matter was an exceedingly ordinary dispute that the complainant and accused could have resolved themselves, not one requiring any of the procedures described above. It was magnified greatly and decided one-sidedly. I earnestly ask friends to investigate calmly why this occurred.

Was the “method of judgment” employed by the Tillai Brahmins compatible with worldly practice? What judgment should an impartial listener have delivered after hearing both sides? Even if Tiruneelakantar's conduct were treated as an offence, could this form of judgment be accepted? Can anyone accept as legal judgment that a man must take his wife by the hand, immerse himself in a pond and swear an oath concerning an offence he is alleged to have committed?

Moreover, that method was not proposed by the arbiters who heard the case. The Brahmin ascetic who brought it demanded this of his opponent Tiruneelakantar. The Tillai Brahmins took the complainant's demand as sufficient and delivered judgment accordingly, while rejecting the defendant's proposal. Tiruneelakantar had offered to provide a good new bowl in place of the missing earthen one. Apparently that modern court considered it contrary to justice to decide upon that proposal.

Let us concede that their view was legally sound. If a thief asks forgiveness by returning what he stole or providing an equivalent, forgiving him may be wrong. Even if the stolen object is later recovered, everyone can accept that he should be punished for undertaking theft. But is it proper, just or orderly for the victim to instruct or request the court to impose a particular punishment, and for the court to decide exactly as asked? Is such a judgment compatible with worldly practice, reason and morality?

I shall now say what the judgment ought to have been. The *Periyapuranam* itself tells us that Tiruneelakantar did not steal the bowl. It did not simply disappear from where it was kept; the giver deliberately concealed it. The judges did not know these facts. They might therefore have told Tiruneelakantar: “Because you did not return the plaintiff's property when he requested it, you are guilty. We fine you ten rupees; in default, you must spend one month in prison.”

Could anyone regard as just an order requiring an alleged offender instead to grasp his wife's hand and immerse himself in a pond?

There is another noteworthy matter requiring investigation. The Tillai Brahmins who heard the case knew Tiruneelakantar well. They knew he was a genuine devotee of Siva, had undertaken the charitable work of giving sacred begging bowls to Siva's devotees, and would not utter a lie even in a dream. Tiruneelakantar and those Brahmins lived in the same town, Chidambaram. He visited their Nataraja temple without fail morning and evening and worshipped the deity crying “Tiruneelakantam! Tiruneelakantam!” Everyone, including those Brahmins, consequently called him Tiruneelakantar.

But they did not know the Brahmin ascetic who brought the case at all. They knew neither his character nor conduct.

If people who thoroughly knew one man's character, behaviour and habits rejected his statement and decided a case according to the complaint of a complete stranger, need one even ask what their judgment was based upon? Anyone who uses even a little reason and investigates impartially will understand that communal attachment produced the unjust decision.

Indeed, examine any episode in the *Periyapuranam* and follow its conclusion. Each event reflects like a mirror that the work was deliberately composed to elevate either a Brahmin or Brahminism in some fashion. The two cases discussed here and the manner in which they were decided are enough to support this claim.

Siva, who came to enslave Sundarar, appears at Sundarar's wedding pavilion and first makes his complaint before those assembled there. The case is later taken to the Brahmins of Tiruvennainallur for judgment. Why was a complaint made before the many people at the wedding not decided by them, but transported to the Brahmins of another place?

Did those at the pavilion lack the qualification and right to hear a case while only the Tiruvennainallur Brahmins possessed them? The purana provides no evidence. Nor does it say that the government of that time had granted only those Brahmins legal authority to decide cases in the region. Why, then, was the complaint made in one place and the judgment completed in another?

The case was taken to a place containing only Brahmins because the wedding gathering included other communities too, and those people might have intervened and condemned the enslavement of human beings by other human beings.

A particular incident supports this explanation. The *Periyapuranam* itself says that when the assembled Brahmins heard the complaint of the Brahmin Siva who came to enslave Sundarar, they rose angrily and cried: “How can a Brahmin be slave to another Brahmin? This is improper!”

The non-Brahmin people present would then have asked: “If there is no custom by which one Brahmin becomes another Brahmin's slave, does that not imply that people of other communities are slaves to Brahmins? Is that proper, fitting or tolerable?” It is therefore clear why the case was removed elsewhere.

Sekkizhar may have imagined that he concealed this undeniable truth and displayed great artistry. Yet he shamelessly disclosed, through the verse beginning “How can faultless Brahmins be slaves to another Brahmin...,” the notion that people of other communities should be slaves to Brahmins. Sekkizhar, said to have been born in the agricultural tradition, failed to recognize the dishonour and degradation this inflicts upon non-Brahmins.

Suppose we excuse him as a man constrained by the conditions of his age and by those for whom people travelled a great distance for a quarter measure of rice. Even after times and ideas have changed, some who associate themselves with today's rationalists and claim to work for the eradication of difference, separation, division, superiority and inferiority among people still earn their living writing rare-word glosses, special explanations, expansions and commentaries upon such puranas.

If that is so, one wonders what benefit comes from blaming Sekkizhar, whose purana rested firmly upon the intention that reformist ideas must never arise in the people's minds.

Kalyanasundaranar, who supplied a rare-word and research commentary upon Sekkizhar's verse, writes: “Castes had stabilized in the age of Sundaramurti Swamigal, and boundaries had been established for them. Under those boundaries, one Brahmin could not be slave to another. It was God's sacred will that caste boundaries be destroyed!”

This explanation reminds me of a story. A man visited a friend. One of the friend's sons stood upon the roof throwing a burning brand. The visitor asked, “Which of your sons is the intelligent one?” The friend replied, “The one standing upon the roof throwing the burning brand is the one who can be considered intelligent among my sons.”

Using that story, I leave readers to decide who among Sekkizhar, who composed the *Periyapuranam*, Kalyanasundaranar, who commented upon it, and Coimbatore Subramanian may be considered intelligent, comparing their wisdom with that of the boy who threw the burning brand and his siblings.

As in Sundarar's case, Brahmins also judged Tiruneelakantar's case. What truth emerges from this? The entire life of people in the age of the puranas stood under Brahmin supervision and control, and such works were written to ensure that this supervision and control would never change.

Thus the politics, royal tradition, ministerial duty, warfare, complaint, testimony and judgment found in the *Periyapuranam* serve only to elevate Brahmins, reinforce religious doctrines that advance their status, and thereby degrade the great Dravidian people. The explanations offered thus far show that it was not constructed in a way that could bring even a little benefit to our people.

From beginning to end, the puranas and epics contain ideas unnecessary to modern life, incompatible with worldly practice and destructive of reason. When people such as Comrade P. Sri try to spread them through the country, it shows either that they remain in darkness, unable to understand how far the world has already changed and continues to change, or that they are alarmed by that transformation.

If the first of these explains their position, wisdom requires them to open the eye of reason, observe the world's transformation, and alter their ideas and actions accordingly. If it is the second, they must at least now understand that neither plots born of alarm nor threats employed in other forms can stop the world from changing.

I have written at such length to make them understand this. I now conclude for the present and ask readers themselves to judge how true Comrade P. Sri's statement is: “On seeing the name *Periyapuranam*, some modern scholars may think it too is merely a purana; but it is an excellent treasury of history.”

15 July 1945.

---NOTES---
The source is the conclusion of a multi-week response to P. Sri's writing in *Dinamani*. Only the installment contained in this canonical file is translated here; no absent earlier installments have been reconstructed.

The stories discussed are the Tiruneelakantar Nayanar episode involving a missing sacred bowl and the Sundarar episode in which Siva claims him as a hereditary slave. Terms and quoted verses are translated according to the article's critical argument while the Tamil source remains verbatim.

The source ends with `ஐ. 15-7-1945`; the isolated `ஐ.` may be an initial or OCR artefact and is not silently interpreted in English.
